    "code": "def generated_loss(batch, model_output, extra):\n    log_pw = batch[\"log_prob_w\"]\n    log_pl = batch[\"log_prob_l\"]\n    weight = batch[\"weight\"]\n    adv_w = batch[\"advantage_w\"]\n    adv_l = batch[\"advantage_l\"]\n    diff = log_pw - log_pl\n    sigmoid_w = 1 / (1 + ops.exp(-adv_w * diff))\n    sigmoid_l = 1 / (1 + ops.exp(-adv_l * -diff))\n    loss = -weight * (adv_w * ops.log(sigmoid_w + 1e-8) + adv_l * ops.log(sigmoid_l + 1e-8))\n    return ops.sum(loss)",
    "theoretical_basis": "By applying a sigmoid transform to the product of advantage signals and the log-probability differences, the loss function shapes a smooth non-linear penalty that strongly encourages correct preference ordering. This reduces saturation effects and zero-gradient issues inherent in linear symmetric losses, improving gradient signal quality and ensuring joint preference compliance. The added small epsilon stabilizes the logarithm to avoid numerical instability."
